  "result": "I've fixed the 500s on POST /checkout, and `npm test` passes (7 of 7).\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` didn't accept prices with thousands separators, such as `\"1,299.00\"`, `\"2,450.50\"` and `\"1,005.99\"`. Its regex failed to match those, and the code then read `m[1]` on `null`. That produced the `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')` seen in the log. I compared the checkout payloads in `logs/app.log` against the 500s. Those three price strings are the only non-plain prices in the log, and they match the failures.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands separators and strips them before parsing.\n- Input that still isn't a valid amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, as the function's own comment promised. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`.\n\n**Regression tests** in `test/money.test.js`:\n- The three prices from the log parse to the right cents.\n- Malformed input such as `abc` and `1,29.00` throws `Invalid amount`.\n- `checkout` totals an order with a comma-formatted price.\n\nI didn't check whether `src/server.js` turns an invalid-amount error into a 400 instead of a 500. That would be a sensible follow-up.",
